k and to deter  further actions  the the key aspect here is nato's  article 5 uh principle the idea that if  one member of nato is attacked that  they're they're all attacked and that's  the basis on which  nato security fundamentally lies and do  you think those unconventional attacks  would amount to something that would  trigger article five the short answer is  no uh article five is about an armed  attack in in nato it is possible uh zany  to imagine cyber attacks that could  reach the threshold of article five  those for example that disabled key  military forces that caused serious  physical damage to infrastructure and it  is possible for cyber attacks to cause  that kind of damage but i would say much  more likely is  for cyber attacks in the cognitive space  disinformation propaganda confusion  things that sort of uh are much lower  down the threshold than those which  would cross article five  but i can see  scenarios in which let's take cyber war  which lethal things happen where it's  unclear whether that threshold's been  reached and if that happens you can  imagine putin testing the strength of  this bond and trying to find fissures  and splits within nato that mean that  actually that article 5 becomes less  credible  it's not something that happens quickly  it happens gradually over a number of  years so if you get a disagreement  between nato members about whether  whether to go with article 5 or not the  whole alliance is weakened  do you agree do you agree that that is a  possibility i mean that's a  fundamentally scary thought i think that  that commitment is only as credible and  robust as people believe it is and so if  putin has recast the map of europe by  changing a regime in kiev to his liking  by changing the status quo in belarus  weakening the position the military  position and the defensibility of the  baltic states and of poland uh perhaps  even by sending refugees flooding into  those states casting a shadow over the  independence of of moldova uh which has  long had an area occupied by russian  forces um  then yes that is a threat to article  five uh one thing that has um has i've  been wondering about is just how much  china knew about gave the nod to or was  surprised by what vladimir putin has  done do you think china is  actively supporting russia i think uh  china is guided by three principles one  is its its alliance with russia against  the united states in particular  uh another is its principle of  non-interference  and the third is its desire  um not to be affected by sanctions and  those three things are impossible or to  pursue all at once at the same time so  it has to make some very difficult  choices and i think in the end the one  that it will sacrifice is its principle  of of non-interference so i think it  will help russia um but there's no doubt  that it's uncomfortable  about all of this and i don't think this  is something that china has engineered  or planned or encouraged russia to do  and let's now turn to de-escalation  because i want to see if there are ways  that or how you think this can be  de-escalated and al-qaeda let's get your  sense of that  do you see  a path  where putin will de-escalate i'm afraid  to say that  i think the only way in which he would  escalate is  if he does  has a sense of real  resistance any sign of weakness and  appeasement  would only invite further attack all the  evidence all the imperial evidence  suggests that only strong response is  likely to stop vladimir putin putin is  not the bravest man he is much more of a  thug  who goes where he's allowed to go so i  think the only way to de-escalate uh in  my view is to build a very serious  incredible set of deterrence and being  able uh to hold that line  this is actually uh sort of decisive  moment um  for europe and for russia itself  uh taking kiev bombing kiev um the  cradle of russian cities you know uh  the center of  ancient civilization is is it's a step  which um  which could h